WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Steps for Calculating a Performance Baseline

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.433
Hello, Mike Harry back with you again, where we’re looking at baselining.

3
00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:13.333
Recall that we took our unit error rate divided by the opportunities.

4
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:16.933
Recognizing that an opportunity here, could be the number of defect categories,

5
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:19.833
could be the number of things that could go wrong, or it could be the number

6
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:22.899
of things that has to go right. And we messed up,

7
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.633
say .05 per unit, four things had to go right per unit.

8
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:31.633
Then that's our opportunity error rate.

9
00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:35.633
Of course multiplying that by a million gives us our DPMO.

10
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:41.499
Now, taking to the fourth step, we take the DPMO,

11
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.500
and we simply enter into a table of Sigma values.

12
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:51.566
We look up the DPMO and we see the corresponding Sigma is 3.74.

13
00:00:51.566 --> 00:00:56.399
In other words, a 3.74 Sigma capability

14
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.633
is equivalent to 12,500 defects per million opportunities.

15
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:04.633
So, it's a way to express it.

16
00:01:04.633 --> 00:01:11.566
The 3.74 is our baseline capability per opportunity.

17
00:01:11.566 --> 00:01:18.432
And it's really pretty much that simple. So, when we look at these different applications,

18
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:23.733
I could say this teller right here is 3.74 Sigma,

19
00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:30.699
but yet another teller, perhaps right here might be 4.2 Sigma.

20
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.700
Or a teller in the background here could be Five Sigma.

21
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.700
So, that would beg the question,

22
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.400
why is this person's baseline significantly higher than this person's?

23
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.766
Well, we would investigate, find out the causes,

24
00:01:45.766 --> 00:01:49.766
and then perhaps standardize on the superior process.

25
00:01:49.766 --> 00:01:54.166
In short, you can't tell where you're going

26
00:01:54.166 --> 00:01:59.299
and how far you've come, without the post in the ground.

27
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:05.833
That's where you've been. What you did. So, you got a baseline from which that benchmark

28
00:02:05.833 --> 00:02:09.133
becomes your guiding light

29
00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:13.133
of how much, and how far you've come, in your improvement effort.

30
00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:17.133
So, that's the simplest case here of baselining.

31
00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:23.399
In this instance, we baseline with discrete data, pass-fail data, good-bad.

32
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:29.300
We can also do the same thing, with a few different steps, for continuous data.

33
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:34.800
Based upon means and standard deviations, and the histograms and so on.

34
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.433
For whether it's in normal data, nonnormal data.

35
00:02:38.433 --> 00:02:42.966
So, we can literally benchmark virtually anything.

36
00:02:42.966 --> 00:02:49.399
We can lay down a baseline for all of the core operations within a business.

37
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.933
Baseline them, determine how much improvement has to occur

38
00:02:53.933 --> 00:02:59.233
and then periodically measure our rate of improvement against the baseline.

39
00:02:59.233 --> 00:03:04.899
Let me say that again. This allows us to set forth a rate of improvement.

40
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:10.366
So, if my baseline is 12,500 defects per million opportunities,

41
00:03:10.366 --> 00:03:13.399
corresponding to 3.74 Sigma.

42
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:18.100
And I want to improve, say that the DPMO rate,

43
00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:23.066
at 30% per year over the next five years.

44
00:03:23.066 --> 00:03:27.766
I can then see the Sigma capability of where I'm going. Where I want to be.

45
00:03:27.766 --> 00:03:33.132
In order to close that gap, that's where we go in, identify projects.

46
00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:39.633
Black belt, green belt projects. Execute those projects to derive that rate of improvement.

47
00:03:39.633 --> 00:03:45.699
So, with that thought in mind, that concludes our discussion on establishing baselines.
